# UPDATED 2026-09-15 (decision log, same date, "Governance reset"): merge execution is
# delegated to the orchestrating Claude session once every one of those gates is genuinely
# green on the exact candidate SHA. Lane/subagents still never merge, and no bypass of any
# required check or review tier is authorized by this delegation.
#
# ATTEMPTED then REVERSED same-day, 2026-09-26 (decision log, "`pal-mcp` becomes the ordinary
# review/audit/report/alignment tool" and the follow-up entry the Opus review of PR #376
# triggered): the paths below were meant to get Code Owner review as a real merge gate,
# narrower than the 2026-09-06 decision's blanket "off". **"Require review from Code Owners"
# was never actually enabled**, and the plan to enable it is now dropped: this repo has one
# collaborator (`ThomasHeinThura`), and every agent session — including this one — acts
# through THAT SAME account's `gh` token. There is no separate identity for "an agent" versus
# "Thomas" for GitHub to check an approval against. Enabling the toggle would not stop an
# agent from approving as Thomas; it would only make every future PR touching these paths
# unmergeable (GitHub refuses letting a sole owner approve their own PR under a zero-bypass
# ruleset), and the obvious-looking fix — adding a bypass actor — would blow a hole through
# all required status checks, which is strictly worse than the status quo. **Never add a
# bypass actor to `protect-main` to work around this; ask Thomas instead.**
